3c942-9b54c | 2026-06-29T11:09:48Z | session:3c942260-311d-4e00-8bcc-204045ea87b3 |  \n **#2389 design pass:** done and grounded in the real code — the clean finding is that UniFFI *eliminates* 3 of the 4 byte-lane "exception" symbols outright (`nmp_app_free`, `nmp_mirror_free_bytes`, and the dispatch JSON-string/`nmp_free_string`) via object + `Vec<u8>` + typed `DispatchOutcome` lifetimes; only the update-sink hot push lane is what #2388 measures. **Codex is reviewing that design now** (your authorized milestone consult). \n  \n **#2388 benchmark:** still in flight. \n  \n I'll report back when the codex feedback and #2388 land, then finalize the #2389 scaffold design and fan out implementation. \n  \n User: [Agent task result: Dynamic workflow "Design+build+review the UniFFI-vs-C-callback byte-push benchmark (#2388)" completed] \n {"design":{"comparison_approach":"In-process Rust A/B microbench replaying ONE deterministic sequence of frame-byte buffers through two transports, so the only variable is marshalling. Lane A (C lane): the real wiring from app_lifecycle_ffi.rs — invoke an `extern \"C\" fn(*mut c_void, *const u8, usize)` through the same `Arc<dyn Fn(&[u8])>` closure shape (UpdateListener), zero-copy ptr/len, PLUS one representative \"shell consume\" memcpy out of the transient slice (mandatory for fairness: the real contract says the slice is valid only for the callback duration, so every real host copies once). Lane B (UniFFI lane): link the REAL `uniffi` runtime crate and exercise its actual lowering path for `Vec<u8>` — `RustBuffer::from_vec` (real alloc + memcpy) + `FfiConverter::<Vec<u8>>::lower` + an indirect vtable call into a Rust-implemented foreign-trait callback stub that performs the lower-bound foreign consume (memcpy RustBuffer contents into an owned Vec, then free), mirroring what Swift/Kotlin do when building a Data/ByteArray. Same buffers, same order, same allocator for both lanes. Subtract a measured empty-harness baseline (timer + index