3c942-a82e4 | 2026-06-29T11:10:44Z | session:3c942260-311d-4e00-8bcc-204045ea87b3 | ADR-0073's "not a museum" rule is about **durable guidance docs** (ADRs, builder-guide) that would otherwise present competing *current* rules. The issue queue is explicitly the opposite — it's the single canonical *temporal* tracker, and it's *supposed* to hold deferred and future work. So "predates the epic" is not, by itself, a reason to remove anything. A healthy post-v1 backlog living in issues is the system working as designed; the alternative (deleting it and trusting memory) is what the planning-discipline rule forbids.
3c942-51485 | 2026-06-29T11:10:44Z | session:3c942260-311d-4e00-8bcc-204045ea87b3 | And critically: **you don't need consolidation to get "what not how."** That's a per-issue body-style choice. A normal issue written as *problem + constraints + open questions* (no prescriptive plan) already gives you exactly that — it's the [[feedback_codex_design_not_prescribe]] pattern. The stale ones are stale because they were written as finished plans, not because they're separate issues.
