3c942-78b3e | 2026-06-29T11:24:47Z | session:3c942260-311d-4e00-8bcc-204045ea87b3 | - **0069 — Explicit feature composition.** Kills `register_defaults()` as production app architecture. A production app's Rust composition root must *explicitly* install substrate, reusable Nostr protocol features, app-owned product features, shell capability contracts, then start. `nmp-defaults` may survive only as a reusable installer library — **never** owning seed follows, bootstrap relay brands, signer permission defaults, or onboarding/product policy. "A request from one downstream app is evidence, not permission" to add app-named helpers to NMP crates. [...] - **0072 — Runtime/capability/shell boundary.** Tightens RMP's "Rust owns durable behavior." Anything a *second platform* would have to reimplement to stay correct (relay choice, signer choice, tag mutation, publish retry, queue truth, nav meaning) belongs in Rust. Browser durable storage (Worker/OPFS) must init before product start — **silent in-memory fallback cannot count as success**. Widgets/AppIntents/CarPlay/Live Activities must not own parallel queues or publish models.
3c942-89f70 | 2026-06-29T11:25:02Z | session:3c942260-311d-4e00-8bcc-204045ea87b3 | A standing **follow-up rule**: a PR touching a folded/amended ADR's implementation area must keep old public-surface counts flat-or-decreasing, or update the owning ADR in place — no new correction docs that leave stale guidance behind. Each architecture slice carries a deletion ledger (old doors deleted/privatized, new concepts, net permanent concepts), and ratchets prevent old-pattern counts from rising. [...] - **Sequencing is actually correct.** My first worry was that the READ slices are all demotions/deletions presupposing a replacement — but #2335 (build the first typed read session) and #2354 (migrate a browser read recipe) are both **closed as of today**. So the build→migrate→delete order is honored; deletions follow landed replacements (NS-M3's "after replacement paths exist"). #2364 even keeps a test proving a typed session still opens. That's the right way to do a clean break.
3c942-c551d | 2026-06-29T11:25:13Z | session:3c942260-311d-4e00-8bcc-204045ea87b3 | ## Gallery is a conformance harness, not a product \n Chirp is a *product* — a real Nostr client. Extracting it proves "you can ship a real app on NMP from outside, on a pinned rev," and it's *fine for Chirp to lag* HEAD. That's why extraction earns its keep. \n  \n Gallery is the opposite. The structure says it plainly: `registry.json` + `showcase-references.json` + `REGISTRY_SECTIONS`, with `android/ios/desktop/tui/web` variants. #2363's own investigation list calls out its responsibilities as "component registry proof, JNI/C-ABI proof, snapshot decoding proof, CI jobs." That's a **cross-platform conformance/regression harness** — a storybook that proves every NMP component decodes and renders on every platform *at HEAD*. Its value is **breadth + currency**, not shippability. \n  \n ## Why extraction actively hurts here \n Pinning Gallery to a git rev in another repo converts a **HEAD-tracking CI gate into a lagging external check**. A framework change that breaks a component's snapshot decode or its JNI lane won't be caught until someone *manually bumps the pin* in the other repo and re-runs it. That is precisely this project's #1 recurring tax — drift, half-migration, dangling pointers (the `nmp-native-runtime` extraction was *just* flagged as incomplete/dual-ownership; "master moves FAST, extractions leave the old path behind"). You'd be institutionalizing that failure for the one surface whose entire job is to catch it. \n  \n **The decisive test:** *when a new NMP feature/component lands, must Gallery prove it in the SAME PR?* Given `registry.json` is meant to be the canonical catalog and Gallery is the multi-platform proof — the answer is almost certainly **yes**. That means it's `examples/`, and `examples/` lives in-tree in every framework (React, Bevy, etc.) for exactly this reason. [...] ## What I'd actually do \n - **No-go / defer**, and lean toward keeping the conformance core in-tree *permanently*. And explicitly **don't extract before #2256** (the clean-room DX gate) — pinning an external repo to a still-moving public surface maximizes drift. #2363 asks "blocks or follows #2256"; my answer is *follows, if ever*. \n - The genuine wins you want from extraction are reachable **without moving it**: \n   - *Decoupling from framework internals* → already most done by #2360 (explicit composition) + finishing the `registry.json`-canonical / delete-hardcoded-`REGISTRY_SECTIONS` work (#8 in your audit list). That makes Gallery a **disciplined in-tree consumer that only touches public surfaces** — ~90% of the "proves external consumability" benefit, zero drift. \n   - *CI/build bloat* (the iOS/Android/desktop builds + checked-in native artifacts are the real cost) → scope/gate those CI jobs and fix the checked-in binaries; don't sever the proof to speed up CI. \n  \n So reframe the "one rule." It isn't "no apps in the framework repo" — it's **products live outside (Chirp), conformance harnesses live in-tree (Gallery)**. Both then follow a coherent rule; they're just on opposite sides of it. \n  \n If you disagree and Gallery is meant to be a *showcase product* you'd actually ship/market (not a dev proof), that flips it back toward extraction — so the one thing worth pinning down first is: **is Gallery a product or a proof?** Everything follows from that. Want me to drop this analysis onto #2363 as the go/no-go evidence it's asking for? [...] User: ok, no-go -- you want to take over the registry.json canonical an delete hardcoded whatevers that you mentioned? \n 
3c942-89f78 | 2026-06-29T11:25:26Z | session:3c942260-311d-4e00-8bcc-204045ea87b3 | ## The principle: the issue queue is the *temporal* surface, not a museum \n  \n ADR-0073's "not a museum" rule is about **durable guidance docs** (ADRs, builder-guide) that would otherwise present competing *current* rules. The issue queue is explicitly the opposite — it's the single canonical *temporal* tracker, and it's *supposed* to hold deferred and future work. So "predates the epic" is not, by itself, a reason to remove anything. A healthy post-v1 backlog living in issues is the system working as designed; the alternative (deleting it and trusting memory) is what the planning-discipline rule forbids. \n  \n So the test isn't age. It's per-issue: **is it still true, still independent, and not contradicted by the reset?** Applying that, the pre-epic issues fall into three buckets:
