3c942-31130 | 2026-06-29T11:25:26Z | session:3c942260-311d-4e00-8bcc-204045ea87b3 | ## The single-prose-backlog-issue is a trap \n  \n The instinct ("don't over-prescribe *how*, since the new arch may change the approach") is exactly right. But a single issue holding "a bunch of text" is, mechanically, **a scattered todo list / parallel roadmap living inside one issue** — which is the precise thing the planning-discipline rule bans, and the thing WIP.md was retired for. It just relocates the anti-pattern from a file to an issue body. Concretely you'd lose: \n  \n - **Queryability** — a prose blob has no `priority:`, `area:`, `phase:` per line. You can't sort it, can't pull "the next p1," can't hand one item to an agent. \n - **PR linkage** — no `Closes #N`. Completion becomes "edit the body and delete a paragraph," with no audit trail and no "closed as completed." \n - **Per-item lifecycle** — you can't tell, at a glance, what's done vs stale vs active inside the blob. It becomes write-only within a month. \n  \n And critically: **you don't need consolidation to get "what not how."** That's a per-issue body-style choice. A normal issue written as *problem + constraints + open questions* (no prescriptive plan) already gives you exactly that — it's the [[feedback_codex_design_not_prescribe]] pattern. The stale ones are stale because they were written as finished plans, not because they're separate issues.
3c942-8bb1e | 2026-06-29T11:25:26Z | session:3c942260-311d-4e00-8bcc-204045ea87b3 | ### C. Verify-and-close housekeeping — decay-prone, doing the least useful work \n **#2298** and **#2299** are both stash-reconcile trackers, and they're the ones most likely to be rotting: \n - **#2299** is auditing stashes for issues that are *already closed* (#2210 native-runtime, #2089 filterless-observers). This is "confirm master already has it, then close" — pure housekeeping that's been open since the 28th. Time-box it and resolve. \n - **#2298** is subtler: it's **p2 pre-v1**, so the *underlying bug* (the `claim→snapshot→render→claim` loop) genuinely matters. But its preserved stash references the **deleted `nmp-wasm` crate** and touches profile-claim/ref-resolution territory that ADR-0063/0070 have since reshaped — so the *stash itself* is partly obsolete by construction. The right move is **not** to babysit the stash; it's to verify whether the loop still reproduces on current master (it may already be fixed, or now belong inside a typed session's replay-before-live semantics), then either file a real fix slice or close with proof. \n  \n ## The one thing not to do \n Don't mass-close by age. That dumps real backlog and violates the single-surface rule. The work is *triage*, not deletion: bucket A stays untouched, bucket B gets a reconciliation comment + governing-ADR pointer, bucket C gets time-boxed to verify-and-close. [...] ## What I'd actually do (your milestone idea, refined) \n  \n 1. **Create a `New Arch` GitHub Milestone.** Put epic #2340 + all `SLICE-NS-*` + #2256 in it. Now `milestone:"New Arch" is:open` is your burn-down — the focused "what we're working on" list, with a built-in progress bar. (The `north-star` label already approximates this; the milestone adds the progress view you're describing.) \n  \n 2. **Fold bucket B into that milestone, re-scoped to "what not how."** #2125 (UniFFI), #2232 (Marmot C-ABI), #2038 (Chirp Web) *are* new-arch work now. Rewrite each body down to problem + the governing ADR as a constraint, strip the stale prescriptive plan. This is where your instinct applies — per issue, not as a blob. \n  \n 3. **Close bucket C, don't archive it.** #2298/#2299 are stash-reconcile housekeeping referencing deleted crates and closed issues. The honest reduction is verify-and-close, not folding into a backlog doc.
3c942-1bab5 | 2026-06-29T11:25:26Z | session:3c942260-311d-4e00-8bcc-204045ea87b3 | **3. One real ordering gap in the WRITE chain.** The dependency graph is *partially* encoded — #2370 and #2372 both declare `Depends on: #2368`. But **#2371 ("delete anonymous explicit-route defaults") declares only `Blocks #2256`**, with no `Depends on` for #2370 (typed signer provenance) or #2369 (finalization gate). Deleting the anonymous route before the typed-provenance replacement is wired is exactly the build-then-delete inversion you avoided on the READ side. I'd add an explicit `Depends on: #2369, #2370` to #2371 so an agent doesn't pick it up first.
