3c942-fec21 | 2026-06-29T11:47:26Z | session:3c942260-311d-4e00-8bcc-204045ea87b3 | The instinct ("don't over-prescribe *how*, since the new arch may change the approach") is exactly right. But a single issue holding "a bunch of text" is, mechanically, **a scattered todo list / parallel roadmap living inside one issue** — which is the precise thing the planning-discipline rule bans, and the thing WIP.md was retired for. It just relocates the anti-pattern from a file to an issue body. Concretely you'd lose: \n  \n - **Queryability** — a prose blob has no `priority:`, `area:`, `phase:` per line. You can't sort it, can't pull "the next p1," can't hand one item to an agent. \n - **PR linkage** — no `Closes #N`. Completion becomes "edit the body and delete a paragraph," with no audit trail and no "closed as completed." \n - **Per-item lifecycle** — you can't tell, at a glance, what's done vs stale vs active inside the blob. It becomes write-only within a month. \n  \n And critically: **you don't need consolidation to get "what not how."** That's a per-issue body-style choice. A normal issue written as *problem + constraints + open questions* (no prescriptive plan) already gives you exactly that — it's the [[feedback_codex_design_not_prescribe]] pattern. The stale ones are stale because they were written as finished plans, not because they're separate issues.
3c942-35224 | 2026-06-29T11:47:40Z | session:3c942260-311d-4e00-8bcc-204045ea87b3 | - **0070 — Typed read sessions.** The app-visible read model is one typed session descriptor+handle owning the *complete* lifecycle (acquisition → route policy → bounded replay → live sink → admission → typed output → wake sources → teardown). `open_interest` is demoted to acquisition-only substrate; `ObservedProjection` and `ReducedSource` become **private machinery**, not app vocabulary. Empty dynamic source sets **fail closed** — never silently become wildcard relay demand. Shells stop hand-authoring filters/projections.
3c942-dbf3e | 2026-06-29T11:47:49Z | session:3c942260-311d-4e00-8bcc-204045ea87b3 | - **0071 — Publish intents & route provenance.** Keeps ADR-0064's single write doorway but separates construction → finalization → signing → publishing as stages of *one actor-owned workflow*. Draft builders are allowed (composable, ergonomic) but must not sign/publish/choose relays as side effects. Every explicit relay route must carry a typed **provenance class** (automatic / host-pin / verified-private-inbox / manual / imported / diagnostic). Private routes fail closed without verified-inbox provenance. Dispatch acceptance/queued state is **never** terminal publish success.
019f0-1cf49 | 2026-06-29T12:10:33Z | session:019f0dc3-5b56-79d1-a14b-5746c93e5879 | Assistant: Yes. I’d do it. But I would **not** do a blind ADR deletion pass yet. \n  \n Live #2320 says the right sequencing: after the #2316 redesign direction is accepted, collapse the ADR set so it describes the architecture we actually want. #2316 is still open and explicitly says the solution belongs in a follow-up ADR, so the next move is not “delete 40 ADRs tomorrow.” The next move is: accept a compact redesign ADR set, then use #2320 to retire/fold the vestigial ADRs. \n  \n My recommendation: \n  \n 1. Create a small current ADR set, probably 4-6 records: \n    - app model and explicit composition; \n    - typed read sessions / output ownership; \n    - dynamic source reconciliation as private machinery; \n    - write flow: construction, signing, publishing, publish ledger, route provenance; \n    - runtime/capability/shell boundary; \n    - maybe storage/replay/sync invariants if they don’t fit cleanly elsewhere. \n  \n 2. Classify every existing ADR into four buckets: \n    - folded into redesign ADR; \n    - folded into another durable owner like builder guide/product spec/architecture doc; \n    - still-current standalone invariant; \n    - retired/deleted because milestone-specific, stale, or superseded. \n  \n 3. Be aggressive about deletion. The directory currently has 68 numbered ADRs plus README, and search still finds current docs teaching `register_defaults`, `open_interest`, `open_feed`, `ReducedSource`, `ObservedProjection`, and old route seams as if they are stable architecture. That is exactly the bad state: too many “accepted” historical records competing with the intended model. \n  \n 4. Keep standalone ADRs only when they protect a live invariant better than the redesign ADR can. Example candidates to keep: fail-closed NIP-42/NIP-44/private-routing invariants, browser/native runtime ownership split, one-byte write boundary, maybe storage/replay invariants. Demo-target, milestone, old feed/open-interest, projection-tier, and defaults-era ADRs should mostly fold or die. \n  \n The principle I’d use: **an ADR directory is not a museum.** Git is the museum. `docs/decisions/` should be the current decision surface. If a new contributor reads it and comes away thinking production `register_defaults()`, app-facing `open_interest`, public `ReducedSource`, or hand-wired `ObservedProjectionSink` is the intended future, #2320 failed. [...] The principle I’d use: **an ADR directory is not a museum.** Git is the museum. `docs/decisions/` should be the current decision surface. If a new contributor reads it and comes away thinking production `register_defaults()`, app-facing `open_interest`, public `ReducedSource`, or hand-wired `ObservedProjectionSink` is the intended future, #2320 failed. \n  \n So: yes, rewrite/collapse. But only after we promote the north-star packet into a compact accepted redesign ADR, otherwise we risk deleting old guidance before the replacement is authoritative.
019f0-aa4b7 | 2026-06-29T12:10:54Z | session:019f0dc3-5b56-79d1-a14b-5746c93e5879 | Assistant: The smallest non-crazy design appears to be: app crates own a local kind/action manifest next to their `.fbs` schemas, and `nmp-codegen` learns to read that manifest instead of only the in-tree `ACTION_BUILDERS` const. NMP does not need to understand the semantic meaning of the custom kind; it needs to generate the boring envelope/builder/binding/drift machinery and let the app’s Rust `ActionModule` own policy. [...] My recommendation for #2408: the right shape is the small static app-local contract path, not a plugin system. \n  \n App-private kind flow should be: \n  \n 1. App owns `action-builders.json` + `.fbs` schema next to its Rust app crate. \n 2. `nmp-codegen` generates Swift/Kotlin/TS typed builders from that app-local registry. \n 3. Native/web calls the generated builder and dispatches bytes through the normal NMP doorway. \n 4. App Rust owns `ActionPayload::decode`, validation, tag/content policy, and `ActionModule`. \n 5. NMP owns only the substrate after that: dispatch envelope, registration, signing, routing, publishing, drift gates.
