898a4-0ec98 | 2026-06-29T12:40:20Z | session:898a41b5-68e0-4b0f-b16c-c6072454bd6a | Assistant: Good question — "door" is just the metaphor for **the app-facing API surface**. The refactor splits the public surface into two doors: \n  \n - **Read door** = how an app *consumes* data → typed read sessions ("given the store, what should the UI see?"). \n - **Write door** = how an app *produces and emits* Nostr events → everything an app does that creates a new event on the network. \n  \n So "write what?" — **Nostr events.** Every user action that isn't just reading is a write: post a note, reply, react/like, repost, follow/unfollow, edit profile (kind:0), send a DM, join/post in a group, delete an event, mute someone. All of those are "writes" the app makes through this door. [...] - **The shell is dumb on purpose.** It builds a typed action and renders status. Routing, signer choice, retries, durable write state, provenance — all Rust. (Thin-shell rule; the same kernel drives iOS, Android, and browser.) \n - **A write is a tracked object with a lifecycle, not a function call.** The whole point of the write-door redesign is that "I published a note" is a durable, observable status fact that survives offline and reports honest terminal outcomes — versus the old model where dispatching and succeeding were conflated.
898a4-dcb20 | 2026-06-29T12:40:20Z | session:898a41b5-68e0-4b0f-b16c-c6072454bd6a | The core doctrine here (one of the owner's standing rules) is **signing and publishing must be orthogonal** — and the old surface conflated a bunch of distinct concepts into loose, ambiguous calls. The write door's job is to cleanly separate the three things that happen when an app emits an event: \n  \n 1. **Construction** — build the event (kind, tags, content). "What is this event?" \n 2. **Signing** — a *generic capability* (local key, bunker/NIP-46, hardware). "Who attests to it?" \n 3. **Publishing** — a *separate, optional, routed* step. "Where does it go, and via what provenance?" \n  [...] **6. Signing — a generic capability, backend invisible.** \n The kernel invokes the signing `CapabilityModule`. Crucially the action doesn't know or care *how* it's signed — local nsec, NIP-46 bunker (remote, async round-trip over the shared relay lane), or hardware. The signer backend is deliberately invisible to the protocol worker. Status advances `pending → signed`. (This is the "signing and publishing are orthogonal" doctrine made structural — and WRITE-003's typed `SignerProvenance` is what names *which* identity is signing, with a structured error if it's unknown.) \n  \n **7. Publishing — a separate, routed, provenance-classified step.** \n Now the publish planner decides *where* this goes and **classifies the route by provenance** (ADR-0071): a normal note routes to the author's outbox relays (kind:10002), a group event routes by its `h`-tag relays, a DM by the recipient's inbox, etc. Status advances `signed → stored → planned → sent`. The route can't be anonymous/defaulted — provenance must be explicit (that's what WRITE-004 closes).
898a4-82a63 | 2026-06-29T12:40:20Z | session:898a41b5-68e0-4b0f-b16c-c6072454bd6a | - **Dispatch ≠ success** — handing an event to the publish pipeline is not the same as it landing. Rust owns a **local publish intent / status fact** (pending → signed → stored → planned → sent → failed/exhausted), so the app gets honest, offline-first write state instead of optimistic fire-and-forget. [...] **4. A local publish intent is created immediately — before anything touches the network.** \n The first thing the kernel does is mint a **status fact** owned by Rust: `pending`. This is the offline-first root — the write exists and is durable *before* a signer or relay is involved. This is why "dispatch ≠ success": the app gets a real status object, not a fire-and-forget. [...] - **A write is a tracked object with a lifecycle, not a function call.** The whole point of the write-door redesign is that "I published a note" is a durable, observable status fact that survives offline and reports honest terminal outcomes — versus the old model where dispatching and succeeding were conflated. \n 
898a4-cccb9 | 2026-06-29T12:40:20Z | session:898a41b5-68e0-4b0f-b16c-c6072454bd6a | - **#2370** — SLICE-NS-WRITE-003: replace raw `signer_pubkey` publish selection with typed signer provenance — `agent-ready`; replace raw optional pubkey in publish commands with typed `SignerProvenance` enum; structured failure for unknown signer. [...] - **#2372** — SLICE-NS-WRITE-005: scope pre-signed publish to imported or protocol-owned seams — `agent-ready`; remove pre-signed publish as a general app write door; retain only for Marmot/verbatim/protocol-owned seams with explicit provenance. [...] - **WRITE-003** ✅ — replace a loose optional `signer_pubkey` with a typed `SignerProvenance` ("which identity is signing, and is it known?"), with structured failure for unknown signers. [...] - **WRITE-005** (PR #2398, red) — restrict *pre-signed verbatim publish* (publishing an already-signed event without re-signing) to protocol-owned seams like Marmot/relay-owned events — it's not a general app write door. \n  [...] The kernel invokes the signing `CapabilityModule`. Crucially the action doesn't know or care *how* it's signed — local nsec, NIP-46 bunker (remote, async round-trip over the shared relay lane), or hardware. The signer backend is deliberately invisible to the protocol worker. Status advances `pending → signed`. (This is the "signing and publishing are orthogonal" doctrine made structural — and WRITE-003's typed `SignerProvenance` is what names *which* identity is signing, with a structured error if it's unknown.) [...] The one *special-case* door is **pre-signed verbatim publish** (`publish_signed_event`): publish an already-signed event without re-signing, routed via the event's own pubkey outbox. That's needed for protocol-owned events (e.g. Marmot/MLS wire events) — and WRITE-005 (#2398, the red PR) is what's restricting it to those protocol seams so it can't be used as a general app write door. \n 
3c942-e6ad9 | 2026-06-29T12:46:53Z | session:3c942260-311d-4e00-8bcc-204045ea87b3 | The 10th symbol, `nmp_app_set_lifecycle_callback`, was **not** migrated. The runtime's lifecycle observer slot (`Arc<Mutex<Option<LifecycleObserverRegistration>>>` in `nmp-core/src/actor/commands/lifecycle.rs`) uses a snapshot-then-call pattern: the actor copies the registration under the lock, releases the mutex, then invokes the callback. There is **no `in_flight` counter + `Condvar` drain gate** like `UpdateListenerGate`/`CapabilityCallbackGate` have. So `set_lifecycle_observer(None)` can return while the actor is past the snapshot and about to call the old closure — wrapping a UniFFI foreign ARC there is a use-after-free. This is the **same structural gap as the C4 `ActionRegistry` case (#2429)**. Per spec I flagged it rather than inventing an unsafe teardown; documented in `runtime/mod.rs`. A drain gate must be added to `LifecycleObserverSlot` in nmp-core before this interface can be exposed. (Consequently there is no lifecycle-callback teardown test, because no callback interface is exposed.) [...] ## The two drain gates added \n 1. **`LifecycleObserverGate`** (`crates/nmp-core/src/actor/commands/lifecycle.rs`) — replaced `Arc<Mutex<Option<LifecycleObserverRegistration>>>` (snapshot-then-call, no drain) with an `in_flight` counter + `Condvar`, copied exactly from `CapabilityCallbackGate`. Two registration paths share it last-writer-wins: C-ABI `LifecycleObserverRegistration` and the new Rust-native `NativeLifecycleObserver`. `set_registration`/`set_native_observer`/`clear` drain in-flight before returning. The invocation uses an RAII guard so `in_flight` balances even on panic. \n 2. **`ResultObserverGate`** (`crates/nmp-core/src/kernel/action_registry/result_observer_gate.rs`) — `ActionRegistry`'s result observer moved off the lock-across-the-call mutex. `deliver_result` now snapshots + increments under the lock then invokes with **no lock held**; added `clear_result_observer`